WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.320
Good afternoon, everyone

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.900
and welcome to this week's CIFAR webinar,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.220
Science is the Exit Strategy.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.650
This is a series of virtual
talks that we're hosting

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.080
to showcase how research
from across disciplines

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.310
from computer science to
population and public health,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.810
economics and anthropology can help

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.370
to inform our public health
and economic response

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:20.993
in the wake of COVID-19.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.180
If you don't know us,

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.730
CIFAR is a Canadian based
global research organization

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.380
and our mission and mandate really are

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.750
to bring the world's
top researchers together

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.250
from around the world
and across disciplines

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.610
to address important
questions facing society.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.170
Like many others across the
globe CIFAR has pivoted quickly

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:43.020
to take action on COVID-19.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.420
We've launched many new
projects, new initiatives

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.640
and we're highlighting
those through this series.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:49.950
My name is Elissa Strome,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.300
I'm the executive director of
the Pan-Canadian AI strategy

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.103
and the associate vice
president research at CIFAR.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.800
It's my distinct pleasure
today to introduce you

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.510
to professor Kim McGrail.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.720
She is a professor at UBC
in the School of Population

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.430
and Public Health and the
Center for Health Services

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.060
and Policy Research.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.490
She is director of
research with UBC Health.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.730
She is scientific director
of Population Health Data BC

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:19.070
and I think most relevant
to our conversation today,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:20.610
she is scientific director

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.880
of Health Data Research Network Canada.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.793
Welcome Kim, thank you for joining us.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.835
Before we begin today's session,

00:01:27.835 --> 00:01:31.770
I wanna take care of a
few housekeeping issues.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.010
There goes a truck outside the window.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.440
Today's session will last
approximately 30 minutes,

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.920
Kim and I will have a conversation

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.130
and then we'll gladly open
it up to your questions.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.300
Please send those questions
in through the Q&amp;A function

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.720
and we'll do our best to answer
as many of them as possible

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.910
at the end of our conversation.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.530
This session is being
recorded and will be available

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.780
on the CIFAR website next week.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.870
So let's jump right in.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.550
Kim, thanks again for joining us today.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.880
It's great to have this
opportunity to talk

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.060
to you about the work that you
and your colleagues are doing

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.350
at Health Data Research Network Canada

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.200
to coordinate and integrate
data across the country

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.720
to advance health research.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:11.900
Let's start the conversation

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.610
by having you describe a
little bit about HDRN Canada,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.363
including its vision and
its partnership model.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.350
- Great, thanks for the opportunity.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.990
It's great to have this
conversation with you today.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.460
So Health Data Research Network Canada

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:30.400
is a newly incorporated, not for profit

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.700
and it's a Pan-Canadian network
that is meant to facilitate

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.810
and accelerate
multi-jurisdictional research.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.930
So our network includes data centers

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:43.190
and other organizations
representing all provinces

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:44.560
and Northwest territory

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.750
and we're working on bringing
in Nunavut and Yukon as well

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.140
plus Statistics Canada

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.040
and the Canadian Institute for
Health Information or CIHI.

00:02:53.543 --> 00:02:55.387
There's a starting position of our network

00:02:55.387 --> 00:02:57.590
and the reason we started working together

00:02:58.530 --> 00:02:59.610
over five years ago,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.380
well before we had any funding
to actually do the kind

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.300
of work we're doing now is

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.120
that we really looked across the country

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.750
and see that we have this
position in the world

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:12.380
of data where the glass
really is 3/4 full.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.250
We've got lots of strength
at the national level

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.600
and in our individual
provinces and territories

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.040
but a lot of difficulty in
actually doing a good deal

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.870
of multi-jurisdictional research

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.330
and we really felt that
if we had just the ability

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:32.240
to add a little bit more,
we could leverage all

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.170
of those great existing resources

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.923
and really promote a lot of
more innovative research.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:42.070
So we're a distributed network
and we have a commitment

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.160
to distributing funding.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.380
We're not creating something central.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.370
We're not trying to
create a single database

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:49.880
across the country

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.250
and part of that is
because we have a principle

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:55.090
of respecting local
context and local policies

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:59.530
and taking comers as they
come without demanding

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.310
that they act a certain
way or be a certain thing

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.400
in order to be part of our network

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.840
and we're leveraging wherever possible.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.270
So we're not trying to build something new

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.930
if something excellent already
exists in the world of data,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.560
data resources and access and so on.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.770
So just very briefly, we
have seven objectives overall

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:15.603
for our network.

00:04:15.603 --> 00:04:17.880
One is to create a data
access support system

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.720
to help navigate
multi-jurisdictional researchers,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.940
so it's one stop shopping,
one form, one request process.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.510
We're working to harmonize
and validate definitions

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.070
for important chronic
diseases and other variables

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:32.330
so we can have apples
to apples comparisons

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:33.680
of data across the country.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.480
We're continuing to
expand the types of data

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:38.690
that are available.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.980
Our bedrock really is administrative data

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.930
and population-based information

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:46.410
but we can link that
to cohorts, to surveys,

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:47.890
to electronic medical records

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:50.370
and other things as they become available.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.850
We're developing technology infrastructure

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.690
to improve the request process

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.300
but also documentation storage, metadata

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.133
and other things really

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.170
and this is an eye towards sustainability

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.640
of the network and the functions.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.910
We're creating supports
for advanced analytics

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:09.880
and infrastructure for data analysis

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.730
and really focusing there
on distributed data analysis

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.710
because we know this is one
of the ways of the future.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.520
We're establishing really
strong partnerships

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.370
with patients, the public
and indigenous communities

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.110
and this is really around
underlining the ethics

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:24.070
of data use,

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:27.120
particularly as data
resources are changing so much

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.480
and building strong governance

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.750
to enable national coordination,
ways of working together,

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.550
we have the strength from everybody.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.510
So you can see that our work
really is around organizing

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:38.790
and making data available.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.030
The technical supports around
that and really a strong focus

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.190
on ethics and engagement

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:47.650
and then of course,
transparency and sustainability.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:49.470
- It's an amazing initiative

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.970
and it's so incredibly
foundational to our ability

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:57.970
to really analyze and assess
and understand the impact

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.850
on health of Canadians across
the country and the work

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:04.070
that you're doing is
clearly a huge step forward

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:06.000
from where we have been in the past.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.030
So just so that we understand
it a little bit more deeply.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.530
So through HDRN,
researchers can access data

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.440
from across provinces and territories,

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.640
from across different health systems

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.900
and even across different data holders

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:21.490
like Statistics Canada.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:23.490
As I said, it's a huge step forward

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.240
and so tell us a little bit about

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.680
what are the key
advantages for researchers

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.700
to having this kind of broad,
multi-jurisdictional access.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.500
Why have we not been able
to do that in the past

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.170
and what are the remaining
challenges that you're tackling?

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:39.003
- Great question.

00:06:39.003 --> 00:06:44.003
So I'll start with why
this has been so difficult

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.820
and really wanna start
there by acknowledging

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:48.440
that not everything is difficult,

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.730
some things have really
been done quite well.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:54.130
So CIHI has a number of
Pan-Canadian datasets

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:56.270
that are available and have
been available to researchers

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:57.510
for quite some time

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.870
and Statistics Canada clearly has amazing

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.346
and rich data resources

00:07:02.346 --> 00:07:04.280
and a research data center network

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.260
that has helped researchers
get access to those

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.800
and of course, a number
of provinces have good,

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.970
really good data and support
for access to those as well

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.210
but having said that

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:19.270
it has been absolutely
a really big challenge

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.030
and really a loss to all of
us that we haven't been able

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.650
to conduct all of the
multi-jurisdictional research

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:28.520
that researchers are trying
to propose and put forward

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:32.220
and Pan-Canadian projects
that extend really just

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.110
beyond the data of a single organization,

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.410
that's where we really
struggle is working together

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:37.663
across organizations.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:42.330
I think there's two kind of
fundamental reasons for this.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:45.220
One is the nature of
our Canadian Federation

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.310
and the tension that that kind of sets up

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:50.450
between provinces and territories

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:51.910
and the federal government

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:54.290
and that's a whole political
science conversation

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:56.420
but I do think it's
important to acknowledge

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:59.430
that that is part of what's going on here.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:01.220
The other is that because provinces

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:04.230
and territories have their
individual responsibility

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.390
for delivery of healthcare
and other kinds of services,

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:10.510
the data they collect, the
way that they're collected

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:13.620
and the technical systems that
they use to collect the data,

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:16.780
even the services that
are covered are different

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.720
and this really creates a challenge for,

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.730
again, the apples to apples comparison

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:24.730
but also the ability just to assemble

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.250
and then use the data appropriately.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:31.340
So having said all of that,
the advantages that come

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.840
from addressing this

00:08:38.196 --> 00:08:41.700
are that we can look at the effect.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.300
It's a natural laboratory in Canada,

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:45.370
we have different policies
and different practices,

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:46.850
so we can learn from those

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.840
if we can compare across jurisdictions.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.440
We can also at a national
level look at the effects

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:56.007
of small populations,
small number of conditions

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:02.260
or people who have had a
relatively unique service

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:05.040
and really at the Pan-Canadian level

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.250
for those small populations

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.980
that's the only way we can
do some of this research.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.090
So we do still have lots of challenges,

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:14.270
including sharing of
data across boundaries

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:15.423
and at this point I'd also say

00:09:15.423 --> 00:09:18.810
that we really haven't had
enough engagement with the public

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.740
around the ethical
issues of doing the kind

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:24.453
of data linkage and analysis
that we really wanna do.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.390
- Great and I know that
you're working hard

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.860
on that front and you
mentioned earlier the work

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.690
that you do in engaging with
indigenous communities as well.

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:36.860
One of the things that just occurred

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:37.890
to me as you were speaking, Kim,

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:42.070
I wonder if one of the other
advantages of integrating

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:44.710
and coordinating access to
data across the country,

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.890
health data in particular
across the country

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:50.270
is really being able
to take full advantage

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:52.620
of the diversity of the
population in Canada,

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:54.690
which is distributed across the country.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.360
Do you see that as another advantage?

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.000
- Oh, yes and this is
probably highlighted really

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.660
with the COVID pandemic
and a lot of the discussion

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:07.640
that's been happening around
vulnerable populations

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:11.050
and communities, race-based
data and that sort of thing.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:12.580
If you really want to be able

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:17.110
to truly understand small
populations, rural populations,

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:21.210
certain kind of racial and
ethnic minority populations,

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:24.850
it's probably much better
to have a larger group,

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:27.500
a larger population being
the Canadian population

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.840
to start from in those cases.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.720
- Great and I know from the very beginning

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:36.320
of the pandemic HDRN pivoted very quickly

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.050
to say what can we do
specifically to understand

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.310
and address COVID-19,

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.140
what can we do to understand
how it's affecting Canadians,

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:45.670
how it's affecting health systems

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:47.460
and the demand on health systems.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:51.590
Can you give us a couple of
examples of what HDRN did

00:10:51.590 --> 00:10:53.910
to address COVID and is
doing to address COVID

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.960
and also maybe some of
the specific projects

00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:00.310
that researchers are leveraging
the HDRN data network

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:01.840
in order to address?

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:05.580
- Yeah, we did recognize
early on that there was a role

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.020
for us, even in a starting
point of sharing information,

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:12.450
making researchers aware and in one spot,

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:14.040
similar to what we've done in other cases

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:16.130
of what data might be available,

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:19.200
in which provinces that
could support COVID research

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:20.260
and on the first instance

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:24.170
that meant documenting where
COVID testing data were going

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:26.390
to be available for research access

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:28.650
and what other kinds of data sets

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:30.660
that those could be linked to

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.780
and so we put together,
it was weekly at first

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:38.990
and we've gone to a little bit
more, less frequent updating

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:41.320
of a briefing note just
to describe where we are,

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:43.410
the new features that are available

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.280
and just more recently,
I think it's just today

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:48.910
that came out, we have
been working on metadata

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:52.330
for the COVID data and in
the coming months I expect

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:55.540
that we'll be increasing
focus on the other data sets

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:57.660
that we might be able to link as we start

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:01.330
to think about research
on the broader effects

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:05.670
of COVID, beyond the
people who've had the virus

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:07.830
to thinking about the broader effects

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:09.400
on communities and individuals

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:12.230
and at this point, of
course, there's no projects

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:13.640
that have gone through the whole process

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:17.550
and been completed
through the HDRN supports

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:20.150
but we've been working
with a lot of researchers

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:24.400
on pursuing potential topics
or studies they might wanna do

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.680
and working with some specific ones

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:31.680
on funded projects now ranging
from emergency department use

00:12:31.732 --> 00:12:33.450
and in that case, we're
working with a team

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.240
that's actually assembling a new data set

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.730
that will be available to others as well

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:41.280
to virtual care, reproductive
care and pregnancy outcomes.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.000
So there's wide range of things.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.560
- So the full spectrum of what we can do

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.360
with the data that we have.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.950
I know as a sort of an
interested stakeholder

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:50.783
in the ecosystem,

00:12:50.783 --> 00:12:54.040
I've really appreciated getting
your COVID-19 briefing notes

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:57.280
on a regular basis to
just maintain my awareness

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:59.760
about what's going on and what's available

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.990
for researchers so that I can share

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:03.963
it amongst my community as well.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.030
So I think we're just about
ready to head into questions.

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:11.790
So I just wanna let the
audience know that we'll head

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.990
to questions after our
next topic of discussion.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:16.700
So if you've got
questions, please put them

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:20.550
into the Q&amp;A box for us and
we'll get to them right away

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:22.780
but the last topic that
I wanna touch on sort

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:25.070
of formally with Kim is the fact

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:27.790
that over the last year, she
and I have worked together

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:30.600
and had the opportunity to
work with 15 other leaders

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:35.330
from across the AI and health
research innovation ecosystems

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:38.320
in a national AI for health task force

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.060
and what we've been doing
is developing a series

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:43.560
of recommendations to both
the federal government

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.170
and provincial and territorial governments

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.003
towards a national AI for health strategy.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.840
The report of the AI for health
taskforce which we've titled

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:55.510
Building a Learning Health
System For Canadians

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:58.170
is now available on the CIFAR website.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:02.260
So Kim, let's hear from you a
little bit about why you think

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:05.530
it's so important that we
have a national strategy on AI

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:09.253
for health and how it will
deliver benefits to Canadians.

00:14:09.253 --> 00:14:11.354
- I really appreciate being
able to talk about this

00:14:11.354 --> 00:14:12.970
because it was such a pleasure to work

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.740
as part of that task force

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.860
and you produced a fantastic report.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.180
So congratulations on that.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:22.570
So I think it's important to start just

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:26.600
from the basics of AI
and just my perspective

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:30.550
on this is AI has like many technologies,

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:33.660
probably like any technology at all,

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.700
it has a great potential for good

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:38.746
but equally can be used to produce harm

00:14:38.746 --> 00:14:42.550
and it could be unintended
consequences, it's still harm.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.390
In addition, AI is one of the drivers,

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.000
is what has been described

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:49.970
as the fourth industrial revolution

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:51.410
and should we kind of summarize

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:53.770
what I think people are
talking about when they see

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:55.770
it's this kind of continuation

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:58.120
of the digital revolution
that's kind of starting

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:01.260
to blur the distinctions between

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.070
or to kind of combine physical, biological

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.560
and computing kind of processes.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:11.180
So man and machine kind
of a framework of things.

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:13.650
So in other words, it's expected

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:17.653
that AI is gonna be really
disruptive to business,

00:15:19.371 --> 00:15:22.500
to governance and to our daily lives

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:25.613
and if we believe this then
I think it's really important

00:15:25.613 --> 00:15:28.790
that we have a coordinated strategy.

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:29.880
First of all, to make sure

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.280
that we truly understand what's happening

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:35.100
and both the unintended
and intended consequences

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.590
and that we have a handle
on how best to make sure

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.070
that we get good things out of AI

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:44.740
and secondly, that we mitigate
any negative consequences

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:46.923
if we are able to do so.

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:50.567
So then if we think more
specifically about health

00:15:50.567 --> 00:15:53.580
and the health care system in Canada

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.320
it makes sense for us to
focus on health in part,

00:15:56.320 --> 00:16:00.210
I mean, we have a ongoing
abiding, respect and love

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.700
for a universal healthcare system with all

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.700
its imperfections, it is
still a great building block

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:08.917
and hopefully we can continue
to make improvements in that.

00:16:08.917 --> 00:16:12.340
The AI approaches in health
can accommodate a large amount

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:14.440
of data, including images,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:17.160
they can help us make sense

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.810
of this vast quantity of information,

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:22.260
it can improve efficiency and diagnostics

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:27.620
and AI systems can be used
to augment human activities,

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:32.370
so they can help make providers
provide better services

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:35.100
but they also could do
things like help people live

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.290
in their homes for longer

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:42.990
and augment those informal
supports as people age.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.940
So ultimately I think we
need a Pan-Canadian strategy

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:50.370
in AI for health because it
is a disruptive potential

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:53.460
of this technology, because of the changes

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:55.390
in the infrastructure that are needed

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:57.030
for AI are significant

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:01.590
and because AI in health is
ultimately a global issue

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:04.730
and if we want to have
some influence on that

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:08.650
at a global level, we need to
work in a Pan-Canadian way.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:09.483
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:17:09.483 --> 00:17:12.020
I think on that last
point, one of the things

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:14.250
that we discussed a lot at the task force,

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:15.650
of course, we talked a lot about data

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:17.310
and data access, you'd recall

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.340
and other members of
the task force I see are

00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:21.440
on this webinar in the audience today,

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:23.540
we spent an inordinate amount

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:25.360
of time talking about data and data access

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:29.860
and the pandemic has
really actually allowed us

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:31.870
as a research and innovation community

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:33.610
to make some important steps

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:36.770
and HDRN has really been
leading the way on that

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:39.230
but the other thing that we spent a lot

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:40.210
of time talking about

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:43.500
at the task force was
really Canada's opportunity

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:46.550
around the application of AI to health

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:48.730
and our specific opportunity

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:52.590
because we are a great leading AI nation,

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:53.930
we have tremendous expertise

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:56.280
in this field across the country,

00:17:56.280 --> 00:18:00.900
because we have such
strong values around AI

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:03.720
and its use in a responsible
and ethical manner

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:05.930
and especially when we're
talking about health

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:08.150
and when we're talking
about personal data,

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:12.090
those principles and values
are so incredibly crucial

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:15.070
but also the opportunity
to really leverage

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:17.580
that publicly funded
health system that we have

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:21.130
and the fact that the
data is and should be

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:24.250
and can be made available
for research and innovation.

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:28.590
So it was such a wide ranging conversation

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:31.660
and we probably could have
had 100 recommendations

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:32.990
in our report and we worked so hard

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:36.013
to really narrow them down
to the focus of the things

00:18:36.013 --> 00:18:40.015
that we felt as a committee
were essential first steps

00:18:40.015 --> 00:18:43.030
in moving Canada forward
to really take advantage

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:45.403
of all of this and bottom line being,

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:47.297
better health for Canadians

00:18:47.297 --> 00:18:49.710
and I think that's the
vision of the task force.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:53.410
So thanks to you for all of
your contributions to that work

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:55.650
and as I said, we've
got a couple of members

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:56.880
of the task force I can see are

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:58.410
on our participant list today,

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.810
so thanks to everybody for their efforts.

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:03.340
So let's move to the
audience questions now

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:07.010
and we'll start with a quick
one actually from Randy Goebel,

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:09.590
a colleague of ours on the task force

00:19:09.590 --> 00:19:11.210
from the university of Alberta

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.840
and from Amii our AI
Institute in Edmonton,

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:16.210
really quick question,

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:19.027
Randy asks, "Is there an EARL

00:19:19.027 --> 00:19:22.967
"that provides information
about our research access

00:19:22.967 --> 00:19:25.240
"to health systems COVID test data."

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:27.210
Randy, I think what you're asking for is

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:30.040
how do you find out what data is available

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:32.467
about COVID testing through HDRN

00:19:32.467 --> 00:19:36.020
and the answer to that is you
can go to the HDRN website

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.870
and it's all on there and
look for their briefs as well

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:40.830
and the brief today actually speaks

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:42.713
to the metadata that's available.

00:19:44.467 --> 00:19:46.543
So another question that's come in.

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:49.237
The participant asks,

00:19:49.237 --> 00:19:53.297
"What are the most expected
worries regarding data sharing

00:19:53.297 --> 00:19:55.460
"from the population and data owners?"

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:58.530
So really the concern that
citizens have and the concerns

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:01.070
that the public have about data sharing.

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:02.320
Can you outline some of that

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:04.253
for us Kim, please?
- Sure.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.560
It's a difficult question
to answer in part

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:10.820
because I don't wanna pretend

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:13.470
to speak on behalf of all Canadians.

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:15.920
What I can speak to is
the existing literature

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:20.920
that has resulted from a pretty
deep public deliberation,

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:24.270
including focus groups but
longer deliberations sessions

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:27.900
as well and I have been
leading some of those

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:31.210
in British Columbia but
there's an international group

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:32.950
of people who've been working in this area

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:35.430
and interestingly, there's quite a lot

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:37.240
of consistency across countries

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:38.470
from what we can see in this.

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:41.830
So people are really, really
open to having their data used

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:46.830
for research but it's always
in context with provisos

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:49.850
and those provisos really revolve

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:53.290
around the Five Safes framework

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:57.340
and this is a framework
that's been emerging as just,

00:20:57.340 --> 00:20:59.430
it doesn't say what needs to be done

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:01.870
but it tells you what
you need to think about

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:05.150
and what those things are is safe people.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:08.950
So who is it that's accessing the data

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:11.700
and people have a lot of
trust for researchers,

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:13.620
university-based researchers.

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:16.110
They start to get a little more concerned

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:18.730
if we're talking about private industry

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:21.150
and it's not, I don't wanna
say that it's a hard no,

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.330
it's just that it changes
the conversation a bit

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.210
and there's different considerations.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:27.270
So safe people is a big one.

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:28.370
Safe environments.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:30.430
Where are you gonna be doing the research?

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:33.560
And increasingly people
expect things to be done

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:37.040
in a controlled, secure
environment because that just says

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:40.260
that you've got greater
control over what's happening.

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:41.280
Safe projects.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:44.440
So what is the question that's being asked

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:46.990
and who's vetted it to say
that it has scientific merit

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:48.740
and will produce some public value?

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:50.850
Safe data.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:54.080
So this is how identifying are the data

00:21:54.080 --> 00:21:56.900
that are going to be used
and have things been done

00:21:56.900 --> 00:21:58.980
to at least remove direct identifiers,

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.290
names and addresses and things like that?

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:03.440
And safe outputs, what's coming out?

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:05.330
What's gonna be shared publicly?

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:08.140
Is there potential for
harm to come to individuals

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:10.990
or communities, small
populations based on that?

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:14.316
So those are the things that
the public worries about,

00:22:14.316 --> 00:22:17.550
less blanket or strict yes or no

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:21.240
and more what's being done to protect data

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:23.423
and put some guardrails around use.

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:27.350
- Great and that Five
Safes framework I found

00:22:27.350 --> 00:22:29.840
in researching this field
and working on the task force

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:33.850
was really such an
excellent conceptual model

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:35.770
to think about what are the things

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:37.200
that we have to be concerned about?

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:38.300
What are the questions that we need

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:40.790
to be asking ourselves as researchers,

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:44.620
as we're considering the
use of individual data?

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:46.720
So along the same lines, we
have a question that came in

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:50.290
from another one of our
participants about the type of work

00:22:50.290 --> 00:22:52.360
that you're doing around community public

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:54.060
and patient engagement.

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:58.010
So he asks you to speak more
to community patient engagement

00:22:58.010 --> 00:23:01.910
in the HDRN and how that
interfaces with the idea

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:05.410
that individual participant
consent is not required

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:07.247
in studies of administrative data.

00:23:07.247 --> 00:23:08.990
"Thank you", he says.

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:09.823
- Yeah, great question

00:23:09.823 --> 00:23:12.420
because of course, again, the bedrock,

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:14.470
not all of the data that
we have is this kind

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:18.520
of what we call unconsented
data but a big portion of it is

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:22.640
and they're unconsented in the
sense that there's allowances

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:26.433
for use of these data in
the absence of consent,

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:31.640
again, with the guardrails around that.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:36.060
So when we are talking about
public and patient engagement

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:38.800
in Health Data Research Network Canada,

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:42.010
we're really focusing on data specifically

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:44.080
and I contrast that with
the broader strategy

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:46.390
for patient-oriented research from CIHR,

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:48.040
which is what we're funded under.

00:23:50.068 --> 00:23:53.400
So this really is a focus on
how are we gonna use data?

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:56.650
What kind of data should we be bringing in

00:23:56.650 --> 00:23:58.830
and what are the limits to allowances

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:01.969
for research uses of that information?

00:24:01.969 --> 00:24:06.969
So we have established an
interim public advisory council

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:10.040
for HDRN, we're just in
the process of recruiting

00:24:10.040 --> 00:24:13.560
and filling out a permanent
structure for that

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:18.560
and we will be looking to
that group for helping us

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:19.790
with a number of things.

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:20.760
One is communication.

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:23.660
So we really feel that
transparency is kind

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:26.590
of the first order of
business for the activities

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:28.600
that HDRN is doing

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:33.060
but another is really to
help truly create a space

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:34.670
where the public has an influence

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:37.120
on the policies around data use

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:41.900
and then I think we will
absolutely take direction

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:43.970
from that advisory
committee on other things

00:24:43.970 --> 00:24:45.470
that we should be considering,

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.530
whether that's about information flows

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:51.120
or about doing things differently

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:52.200
and I do wanna emphasize

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:57.020
that I personally feel
very committed to the fact

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:59.050
that the public needs to have a true say

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:02.620
and this is not just a PR
communications exercise.

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:05.640
- Absolutely and it can't
be tokenism in any way.

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:08.720
It has to be really deep engagements,

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:11.470
consideration, collaboration
with the public

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:14.260
and with patients and we
articulated that very strongly

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:15.570
in the taskforce report,

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:18.690
very much taking inspiration from the work

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:20.000
that you've done at HDRN and

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:22.260
that others have done across
the country on this topic,

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:27.260
really thinking about the role
of citizens in understanding

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:30.780
and contributing to
this work going forward.

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:34.150
I was always inspired by
the UK where they seem

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:38.510
to have a very strong
culture of data trust

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:40.990
and I think that's something
that we can learn a lot from

00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:42.460
and aspire to build in Canada,

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:46.270
this culture of inclusion
within the governance,

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:48.160
within decision making around data

00:25:48.160 --> 00:25:50.140
and that's how you engender trust

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:53.760
and how you're able to
advance research objectives.

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:57.607
Another couple of quick
questions about HDRN,

00:25:57.607 --> 00:25:58.707
another colleague asks,

00:25:58.707 --> 00:26:02.040
"Are there any plans to reach
out beyond our boundaries

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:04.660
to coordinate with other countries?"

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:09.660
Yes as a matter of fact, we
have a partnership around COVID

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:13.950
with the UK Health Data Research group

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.600
and we're working on a
memorandum of understanding

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:18.750
for larger work with them.

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:21.000
In fact, we're working
with them on metadata now,

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.850
because with the idea being

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:27.240
that metadata being how we
describe the data that we have,

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:28.880
if we can align metadata

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:31.730
then we are effectively
aligning the potential

00:26:31.730 --> 00:26:34.550
for international
collaborative research project.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:35.910
So we're working with them so

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:38.703
that we can get those things in place now.

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:41.810
- I think it's such a great partner for us

00:26:41.810 --> 00:26:43.280
because they have so many similarities

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:46.960
in their health system, in
their principles, values,

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.170
democracy and have been a
real leader in this space,

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:52.970
so I think that UK is always
a great example to look to.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.600
- Yep.
- Last question about HDRN

00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:58.423
and then we'll start to wrap up.

00:26:59.390 --> 00:27:01.270
For provinces who are looking

00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:04.720
to build their own internal
capacity for data collection

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:08.390
and sharing can or does
the HDRN assist them

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:11.060
in accelerating those improvements?

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:12.490
- Oh, it's such a great question.

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:13.393
Yeah, it's one of the things

00:27:13.393 --> 00:27:15.900
that we have found over
the last couple of years

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:17.810
of really working together more formally,

00:27:17.810 --> 00:27:20.820
it's one of the greatest
assets we can provide

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:24.090
is all the learning and information

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:27.220
from the existing centers to
share with those who are trying

00:27:27.220 --> 00:27:30.280
to start up but honestly, equally it goes

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:31.550
in the other direction as well.

00:27:31.550 --> 00:27:35.460
So we have established, for
example a privacy working group

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.890
of all the privacy leads
from our organizations

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:39.490
across the country and they're helping

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:42.670
to do a number of things
on legislative review

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:43.860
and that sort of thing.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:46.720
So absolutely those resources are there

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:49.493
and we're really open
to helping as we can.

00:27:50.355 --> 00:27:54.120
- Awesome, so this has been
a fabulous conversation.

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:55.750
Thank you, Kim for joining me today,

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:59.020
it's gone by very quickly
as these always do

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:02.100
and I'm afraid we don't have
time for any further questions.

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:03.480
I wanna remind the audience

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:07.070
that the AI for health taskforce
report is now available

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:10.880
on the CIFAR website and
before you sign off I'd like

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:13.030
to thank everybody for joining us today

00:28:13.030 --> 00:28:16.630
for this conversation and I'd
especially like thank you, Kim

00:28:16.630 --> 00:28:19.240
for telling us the story of HDRN Canada

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:23.750
and the amazing foundation
that it has laid to integrate

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:25.400
and coordinate data across the country

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:28.020
and make it available for research.

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:31.570
CIFAR's virtual talk series
